y>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}},{"name":"stdout","text":"<class 'pandas.core.frame.DataFrame'>\nRangeIndex: 212 entries, 0 to 211\nData columns (total 5 columns):\n #   Column          Non-Null Count  Dtype \n---  ------          --------------  ----- \n 0   constructorId   212 non-null    int64 \n 1   constructorRef  212 non-null    object\n 2   name            212 non-null    object\n 3   nationality     212 non-null    object\n 4   url             212 non-null    object\ndtypes: int64(1), object(4)\nmemory usage: 8.4+ KB\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":5},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"`constructors` has one row per constructor (team), 212 in total:\n\n- **constructorId:** the number that identifies each constructor. Other tables use it to say which team a driver raced for.\n- **constructorRef:** a short text name for the team (e.g. `toro_rosso`), another identifier.\n- **name:** the team's name (e.g. McLaren, BMW Sauber).\n- **nationality:** the team's nationality, written as an adjective (British, German, French, Italian).\n- **url:** the team's Wikipedia page.\n\n**What we notice**\n\n1) **Types:** only `constructorId` was read as a number (`int64`). The other four columns are text (`object`), which is expected since they hold names and links. For text columns, the type cannot tell us whether a `\\N` is hidden inside; the audit checks that.\n\n2) **212 constructors** is far more than the ten or so teams on a modern grid. This fits the F1 introduction's description of earlier eras, with many privately entered cars, and of teams changing names when they are sold (e.g. Toleman became Benetton, then Renault, Lotus and Alpine). According to the introduction, each name gets its own `constructorId`, so a team's history breaks at every rename. A constructor form feature built from `constructorId` will therefore restart at each rename; we accept this and note it as a limitation.\n\n3) **constructorId** is needed for Question 3, which compares mechanical-retirement rates between constructors, and `name` lets us report the answer with readable team names.\n\n4) **nationality** is written as an adjective (\"British\"), while circuits used country names (\"UK\"). Question 2 is about the driver's home country, not the team's, so this column is not needed.\n\n5) **Available before the race?** Yes. Which team a constructor is does not change during a race, so nothing in this table is leakage. `constructorRef` and `url` are descriptive and not needed for any of our questions.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Do renamed teams really get new IDs?** The F1 introduction gives one team's history as an example: Toleman became Benetton, then Renault, Lotus and Alpine. We look up these names to see how the table records them.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"df = raw[\"constructors\"]\ndf[df[\"name\"].str.contains(\"Toleman|Benetton|Renault|Lotus|Alpine\")][[\"constructorId\", \"constructorRef\", \"name\"]]","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-14T10:35:52.303845Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-14T10:35:52.304095Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-14T10:35:52.338424Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-14T10:35:52.304072Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-14T10:35:52.337625Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":6,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"     constructorId  constructorRef                       name\n3                4         renault                    Renault\n21              22        benetton                   Benetton\n31              32      team_lotus                 Team Lotus\n51              53         toleman                    Toleman\n169            172    lotus-climax               Lotus-Climax\n170            173  lotus-maserati             Lotus-Maserati\n173            176       lotus-brm                  Lotus-BRM\n174            177  lotus-borgward             Lotus-Borgward\n177            180      lotus-ford          