, Rosberg and Alonso do. Our guess is that permanent numbers only exist for more recent drivers, which the audit will measure. We do not need this column anyway.\n\n2) **dob** is a date stored as text, so it has to be converted to a real date in cleaning (semantic typing). It could be useful later: a driver's age on race day is known before the race starts.\n\n3) **nationality** confirms what the F1 introduction said: drivers' nationality is an adjective (\"British\"), while `circuits.country` uses country names (\"UK\"). For Question 2 we will need a mapping between the two, so we list all the nationality values below.\n\n4) **Available before the race?** Yes. A driver's identity, birth date and nationality are fixed facts known in advance. `forename` and `surname` are useful for readable output; `driverRef`, `code` and `url` are not needed for our questions, and we always identify drivers by `driverId`.\n\n**How are nationalities written?** To plan the mapping for Question 2, we list every distinct value.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"print(df[\"nationality\"].nunique(), \"different nationality values\")\nprint(sorted(df[\"nationality\"].unique()))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-14T10:35:52.369527Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-14T10:35:52.369869Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-14T10:35:52.375372Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-14T10:35:52.369836Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-14T10:35:52.374666Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"43 different nationality values\n['American', 'American-Italian', 'Argentine', 'Argentine-Italian', 'Argentinian ', 'Australian', 'Austrian', 'Belgian', 'Brazilian', 'British', 'Canadian', 'Chilean', 'Chinese', 'Colombian', 'Czech', 'Danish', 'Dutch', 'East German', 'Finnish', 'French', 'German', 'Hungarian', 'Indian', 'Indonesian', 'Irish', 'Italian', 'Japanese', 'Liechtensteiner', 'Malaysian', 'Mexican', 'Monegasque', 'New Zealander', 'Polish', 'Portuguese', 'Rhodesian', 'Russian', 'South African', 'Spanish', 'Swedish', 'Swiss', 'Thai', 'Uruguayan', 'Venezuelan']\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":8},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"There are 43 nationality values, and three things matter for Question 2:\n\n1) **The same nationality written two ways:** `Argentine` and `Argentinian ` (with a trailing space) describe the same nationality, so they would be counted as two. In cleaning we merge them into `Argentine`, the form also used in `Argentine-Italian`, and remove stray spaces from text values.\n\n2) **Dual nationalities:** `American-Italian` and `Argentine-Italian` combine two countries. When we answer Question 2 we have to decide which country counts as home for these drivers; the simplest rule is to count a race as a home race if it is held in either country. \n\n3) **Nationalities without a circuit:** some nationalities belong to countries that never hosted a race in our circuits list, or no longer exist (e.g. `Rhodesian`, `Liechtensteiner`). Drivers of these nationalities can never have a home race, which Question 2 has to take into account.\n\n   **How many East German drivers are there?** `East German` is a real historical nationality, not a spelling error, so we keep it in cleaning. Whether these drivers count as racing at home in Germany is a decision for Question 2, and its importance depends on how many drivers it concerns.\n\nOverall, drivers use 43 adjective forms while circuits use 35 country names (some as short forms like `UK`), so Question 2 needs a mapping from each nationality to the way its country is written in `circuits` (e.g. British → UK, American → USA, Dutch → Netherlands). We build it when answering Question 2.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"df[df[\"nationality\"] == \"East German\"][[\"driverId\", \"forename\", \"surname\", \"dob\"]]","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-14T10:35:52.376521Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-14T10:35:52.376856