>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>1:25.664</td>\n      <td>1:25.452</td>\n      <td>1:27.079</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>3</th>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>18</td>\n      <td>13</td>\n      <td>6</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>1:25.994</td>\n      <td>1:25.691</td>\n      <td>1:27.178</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>4</th>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>18</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>1:25.960</td>\n      <td>1:25.518</td>\n      <td>1:27.236</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":15},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"`qualifying` has 10,494 rows. From the first rows, each row looks like one driver's qualifying session at one race:\n\n- **qualifyId:** a number identifying each row.\n- **raceId / driverId / constructorId:** the race, the driver and the team the row belongs to.\n- **number:** the car number used in that race.\n- **position:** the driver's final qualifying position (1 = pole position, the fastest).\n- **q1 / q2 / q3:** the driver's best lap time in each part of qualifying, written as minutes:seconds (e.g. `1:26.572`).\n\n**What we notice**\n\n1) **Types:** the six ID and number columns are whole numbers, so they contain no placeholder. `q1`, `q2` and `q3` are text, but here that is expected even without placeholders, because `1:26.572` is not a plain number. The brief asks us to convert lap-time strings like these into seconds in cleaning.\n\n2) **Position follows the last part of qualifying:** the five drivers are ordered by their `q3` times (1:26.714 up to 1:27.236), not by `q1` (driver 5 had the fastest `q1` but qualified 3rd). This matches the F1 introduction's description of the three-part knockout format:\n    - **Q1:** all drivers compete, and the slowest are knocked out and assigned the last starting positions.\n    - **Q2:** the remaining drivers compete, and the slowest of this group are knocked out and assigned the middle starting positions.\n    - **Q3:** the ten fastest drivers compete for pole position. Their final rank (1st to 10th) is decided only by their fastest lap in Q3, regardless of how fast they were in Q1 or Q2.\n\n    For drivers knocked out earlier, we therefore expect `q2` and `q3` to be empty, which we check below.\n\n3) **Qualifying position is not the starting position:** the introduction gives two reasons why `position` here and the `grid` column in `results` can differ: grid penalties can move a driver back, and in 2021–2022 the sprint result, not qualifying, set the grid on sprint weekends. Question 1 asks us to distinguish the two explicitly.\n\n4) **Coverage:** this table has 10,494 rows, only about 39% of the 26,759 in `results`. If every race had qualifying data for every driver, the two would be about the same size, so qualifying must be missing for many races; counting them below confirms that only 494 of the 1,125 races (44%) have any qualifying record. This fits the brief's warning about \"historical qualifying-coverage drift\" and the introduction's statement that qualifying is only recorded in detail for recent decades. Which seasons are covered is measured in the EDA (1.4), and it decides whether qualifying-based features can be used for all races or only some.\n\n5) **Available before the race?** Yes. Qualifying happens before the race, and our prediction cutoff is defined as \"after qualifying\", so this is exactly the kind of information our features can use, as long as we account for its gaps.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"print(\"Races with qualifying data:\", df[\"raceId\"].nunique(), \"of\", len(raw[\"races\"]))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-14T10:56:17.823835Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-14T10:56:17.826922Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-14T10:56:17.846688Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-14T10:56:17.826868Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-14T10:56:17.845055Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Races with qualifying data: 494 of 1125\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":17},{"cell_type":"code","source":"for col in [\"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\"]:\n    print(col, \"| \\\\N:\", (df[col] == \"\\\\N\").sum(), \"| empty:\", (df[col] == \"\").sum())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-14T10:35:52.555929Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-14T10:35:52.556237Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-14T10:35:52.575724Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-14T10:35:52.556204Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-14T10:35:52.574875Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"q1 | \\N: 156 | empty: 0\nq2 | \\N: 4625 | empty: 22\nq3 | \\N: 6819 | empty: 46\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":16},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The counts match the knockout format: `q1` is almost always filled (156 `\\N`), `q2` is missing more often (4,625 `\\N`) and `q3` most often (6,819 `\\N`), since fewer drivers reach each later part. The shares are higher than a modern weekend alone would give (with about 20 drivers, roughly 5 miss Q2 and 10 miss Q3), which fits the introduction's note that before 2006 qualifying was a single session, recorded mostly as `q1` only.\n\nThis means a missing `q2` or `q3` does not always mean the same thing: from 2006 it means the driver was knocked out in an earlier part, which says something about their pace, while before 2006 it means that part of qualifying did not exist yet. We keep this in mind if lap times are used as features.\n\nThe check also shows a second placeholder: besides `\\N`, `q2` and `q3` contain completely empty values (22 and 46). In cleaning, both `\\N` and empty values must be treated as missing; checking for `\\N` alone would have missed them.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**sprint_results**","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"df = raw[\"sprint_results\"]\ndf.info()\ndisplay(df.head())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.sta