2</th>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>1061</td>\n      <td>822</td>\n      <td>131</td>\n      <td>77</td>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>17</td>\n      <td>+7.502</td>\n      <td>1545928</td>\n      <td>17</td>\n      <td>1:29.958</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>3</th>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>1061</td>\n      <td>844</td>\n      <td>6</td>\n      <td>16</td>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>17</td>\n      <td>+11.278</td>\n      <td>1549704</td>\n      <td>16</td>\n      <td>1:30.163</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>4</th>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>1061</td>\n      <td>846</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>6</td>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>17</td>\n      <td>+24.111</td>\n      <td>1562537</td>\n      <td>16</td>\n      <td>1:30.566</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":18},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"`sprint_results` has 360 rows. From the first rows, each row is one driver's result in one sprint race:\n\n- **resultId:** a number identifying each row.\n- **raceId / driverId / constructorId:** the race weekend, the driver and the team the row belongs to.\n- **number:** the car number.\n- **grid:** the driver's starting position in the sprint.\n- **position / positionText / positionOrder:** the finishing position, written in three different ways; the F1 introduction explains the difference, which we look at in `results`, where our target comes from.\n- **points:** points scored in the sprint (3, 2, 1, 0 in the first rows, far fewer than in a full race).\n- **laps:** laps completed (17 here, a short race, matching the introduction's description of a sprint of about 100 km).\n- **time:** the winner's total time (`25:38.426`), and for everyone else the gap to the winner (`+1.430`).\n- **milliseconds:** each driver's total time in milliseconds.\n- **fastestLap / fastestLapTime:** the lap on which the driver set their fastest lap, and that lap's time.\n- **statusId:** why the driver's sprint ended (points to `status`).\n\n**What we notice**\n\n1) **Types:** ten columns are whole numbers. `position`, `milliseconds` and `fastestLap` look like numbers but are stored as text, which suggests a `\\N` somewhere in them; the audit will count them.\n\n2) **One column, two formats:** `time` holds a total time for the winner but a gap (\"+1.430\") for everyone else, so it cannot be converted to seconds with one rule. `milliseconds` holds the same information as one consistent number: the second driver's 1,539,856 ms is exactly the winner's 1,538,426 ms plus 1.430 seconds. If race times are ever needed, `milliseconds` is the column to use.\n\n3) **Available before the race?** Yes. As discussed in 1.0, the sprint finishes before the main race starts, so by the brief's definition of leakage its result is not future information. Whether it is useful depends on how many races have a sprint, and in which seasons, which we check below.\n\n**Which seasons have sprints?** We look up the year of every race that has sprint results.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"sprint_races = raw[\"races\"][raw[\"races\"][\"raceId\"].isin(df[\"raceId\"])]\nprint(\"Races with a sprint:\", len(sprint_races))\nprint(sprint_races[\"year\"].value_counts().sort_index())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-14T11:04:57.404272Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-14T11:04:57.405097Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-14T11:04:57.426007Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-14T11:04:57.405057Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-14T11:04:57.424824Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Races with a sprint: 18\nyear\n2021    3\n2022    3\n2023    6\n2024    6\nName: count, dtype: int64\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":19},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Only 18 of the 1,125 races (under 2%) have a sprint, all between 2021 and 2024: 3 in 2021, 3 in 2022 and 6 in each of 2023 and 2024. With 360 rows, that is exactly 20 drivers per sprint, so the races that have one are fully recorded.\n\nThis makes the sprint of little use as a feature, even though it is allowed. If we train on older seasons, as the introduction suggests (training up to 2019), the training data would contain no sprints at all, so a model could not learn anything from them; for every other race the value would simply be missing. In addition, according to the introduction, in 2021–2022 the sprint result set the starting grid of the main race, so for those races its information is already reflected in the `grid` column of `results`, which we open next. We therefore do not plan to use sprint results as a feature and confirm this when choosing features in Section 3.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**results**","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"df = raw[\"results\"]\ndf.info()\ndisplay(df.head())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-14T11:25:33.638864Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-14T11:25:33.639677Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-14T11:25:33.677906Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-14T11:25:33.639641Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-14T11:25:33.677104Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"<class 'pandas.core.frame.DataFrame'>\nRangeIndex: 26759 entries, 0 to 26758\nData columns (total 18 columns):\n #   Column           Non-Null Count  Dtype  \n---  ------           --------------  -----  \n 0   resultId         26759 non-null  int64  \n 1   raceId           26759 non-null  int64  \n 2   driverId         26759 non-null  int64  \n 3   constructorId    26759 non-null  int64  \n 4   number           26759 non-null  object \n 5   grid             26759 non-null  int64  \n 6   position         26759 non-null  object \n 7   positionText