d>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>11</td>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>5.0</td>\n      <td>58</td>\n      <td>+17.181</td>\n      <td>5707797</td>\n      <td>58</td>\n      <td>7</td>\n      <td>1:28.603</td>\n      <td>215.464</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>4</th>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>18</td>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>23</td>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>4.0</td>\n      <td>58</td>\n      <td>+18.014</td>\n      <td>5708630</td>\n      <td>43</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>1:27.418</td>\n      <td>218.385</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":21},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"`results` has 26,759 rows. From the first rows, each row looks like one driver's result in one race, which is our unit of analysis. The brief warns that this does not always hold: in early F1, drivers sometimes shared a car during a race, so the same driver can appear more than once in the same race. We investigate and fix this later.\n\n- **resultId:** a number identifying each row.\n- **raceId / driverId / constructorId:** the race, the driver and the team the row belongs to.\n- **number:** the car number.\n- **grid:** the driver's starting position in the race.\n- **position:** the official finishing position and its empty if not classified.\n- **positionText:** the finishing position written as text.\n- **positionOrder:** a ranking of every driver in the race; our target is `positionOrder ≤ 3`.\n- **points:** championship points scored in the race.\n- **laps:** laps completed.\n- **time / milliseconds:** the winner's total race time (`1:34:50.616`) and, for the others, the gap to the winner (`+5.478`); `milliseconds` holds each driver's total time as one number.\n- **fastestLap / rank / fastestLapTime / fastestLapSpeed:** the lap on which the driver set their fastest lap, how that lap ranked in the race, its time and its average speed.\n- **statusId:** why the driver's race ended (points to `status`).\n\n**What we notice**\n\n1) **The target and the grid are complete:** `positionOrder` and `grid` are whole numbers, so neither contains a placeholder. This supports the F1 introduction's statement that `positionOrder` is filled for every row, which is why our target uses it rather than `position`. Since `position` is stored as text while showing numbers in the first rows, some of its values are not numbers; we look at these below since postion is for classified only.\n\n2) **Starting position matters but does not decide everything:** in the first race shown, the top three started 1st, 5th and 7th, and the driver who finished 4th started 11th. So `grid` should be a strong feature, but not a perfect one because of DSR\n\n3) **Almost everything here is post-race:** for the race a row describes, only a few columns are known before it starts.\n\n| Known before the race | Only known after the race (leakage) |\n|---|---|\n| `raceId`, `driverId`, `constructorId`, `number`, `grid` | `position`, `positionText`, `points`, `laps`, `time`, `milliseconds`, `fastestLap`, `rank`, `fastestLapTime`, `fastestLapSpeed`, `statusId` |\n\n`positionOrder` is also post-race, but it has a special role: it is where our target comes from (`positionOrder ≤ 3`), so it is never a feature.\n\nThe right-hand columns can still be used for *earlier* races, for example to measure a driver's recent form, which is how we build leakage-safe features in Section 3.\n\n**What do `position`, `positionText` and `positionOrder` show when a driver is not classified?** We look at the rows where `position` is `\\N`.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"not_classified = df[df[\"position\"] == \"\\\\N\"]\nprint(\"Rows without an official position:\", len(not_classified), \"of\", len(df))\nprint(not_classified[\"positionText\"].value_counts())\ndisplay(not_classified[[\"raceId\", \"driverId\", \"grid\", \"position\", \"positionText\", \"positionOrder\"]].head())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-14T12:30:57.404517Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-14T12:30:57.40491Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-14T12:30:57.427754Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-14T12:30:57.40488Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-14T12:30:57.427038Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Rows without an official position: 10953 of 26759\npositionText\nR     8897\nF     1368\nW      336\nN      190\nD      151\nE        9\n11       1\n14       1\nName: count, dtype: int64\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"    raceId  driverId  grid position positionText  positionOrder\n8       18         9     2       \\N            R              9\n9       18        10    18       \\N            R             10\n10      18        11    19       \\N            R             11\n11      18        12    20       \\N            R             12\n12      18        13     4       \\N            R             13","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>raceId</th>\n      <th>driverId</th>\n      <th>grid</th>\n      <th>position</th>\n      <th>positionText</th>\n      <th>positionOrder</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>8</th>\n      <td>18</td>\n      <td>9</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>\\N</td>\n      <td>R</td>\n      <td>9</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>9</th>\n      <td>18</td>\n      <td>10</td>\n      <td>18</td>\n      <td>\\N</td>\n      <td>R</td>\n      <td>10</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>10</th>\n      <td>18</td>\n      <td>11</td>\n      <td>19</td>\n      <td>\\N</td>\n      <td>R</td>\n      <td>11</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>11</th>\n      <td>18</td>\n      <td>12</td>\n      <td>20</td>\n      <td>\\N</td>\n      <td>R</td>\n      <td>12</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>12</th>\n      <td>18</td>\n      <td>13</td>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>\\N</td>\n      <td>R</td>\n      <td>13</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":27},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"10,953 of the 26,759 rows (41%) have no official `position`, far too many to build our target on. In these rows `positionText` holds the letter codes the F1 introduction describes: R (retired, 8,897), F (failed to qualify, 1,368), W (withdrawn, 336), N (not classified, 190), D (disqualified, 151) and E (excluded, 9). `positionOrder`, meanwhile, still gives every driver a rank: the driver who started 2nd and retired is ranked 9th. This confirms why our target uses `positionOrder`.\n\nThe F and W rows are drivers who never started the race; how to handle them is decided in the scope filters (Part B). The check also shows two inconsistent rows, where `positionText` is a number (11 and 14) but `position` is `\\N`. They do not affect our target, which uses `positionOrder`, so we only note them.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How often does each reason for ending a race occur?** `status` lists 139 possible reasons, but not how often each one happens. We attach the reason in words to every result row and plot the most common ones.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# attach the status text to every result (results -> status through statusId)\nres_status = df.merge(raw[\"status\"], on=\"statusId\", how=\"left\")\nprint(\"Rows before and after the join:\", len(df), len(res_status))\n\ntop = res_status[\"status\"].value_counts().head(20)\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 6))\ntop.sort_values().plot(kind=\"barh\", ax=ax)\nax.set_title(\"The 20 most common reasons a driver's race ended\")\nax.set_xlabel(\"number of result rows\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-14T12:01:52.706556Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-14T12:01:52.707503Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-14T12:01:52.98977Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-14T12:01:52.707465Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-14T12:01:52.988731Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Rows before and after the join: 26759 26759\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 800x600 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":24},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The join kept all 26,759 rows, so every result has exactly one reason attached.\n\nThree things stand out. First, finishing dominates: `Finished` is the most common status and `+1 Lap` the second, ahead of every retirement reason, with `+2` to `+5 Laps` also in the top 20. Treating lapped drivers as retirements would therefore misclassify one of the largest groups in the data. Second, the most common retirement reason is `Engine`, followed by both crashes (`Accident`, `Collision`, `Spun off`) and other car failures (`Gearbox`, `Suspension`, `Transmission`, `Electrical`, `Brakes`, `Clutch`), so mechanical retirements are frequent enough for Question 3 to compare constructors meaningfully. Third, non-starters (`Did not qualify`, `Did not prequalify`, `Withdrew`) form a visible group, which we handle in the scope filters.\n\nThe reasons we found hard to classify, such as `Collision damage` and `Retired`, do not appear in the top 20, so each is rarer than the 20th reason shown. How we assign them in Question 3 can therefore only have a small effect on its answer, although we recheck this for the 2014–2024 seasons the question covers.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Do `qualifying` and `results` agree on each driver's team?** Both tables record a `constructorId` for every driver in a race. We will later combine them through the race and the driver, so we check whether the team recorded for the same driver in the same race is the same in both.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Keep only race, driver and team from each table\nquali_teams = raw[\"qualifying\"][[\"raceId\", \"driverId\", \"constructorId\"]]\nresult_teams = df[[\"raceId\", \"driverId\", \"constructorId\"]]\n\n# Put the two teams side by side for the same driver in the same race\nboth = quali_teams.merge(result_teams, on=[\"raceId\", \"driverId\"], suffixes=(\"_quali\", \"_result\"))\nprint(\"Pairs compared:\", len(both))\n\n# Keep the pairs where the two tables name a different team\ndifferent = both[both[\"constructorId_quali\"] != both[\"constructorId_result\"]]\nprint(\"Pairs with a different team:\", len(different))\ndisplay(different)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-14T12:18:14.114435Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-14T12:18:14.115329Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-14T12:18:14.140496Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-14T12:18:14.11529Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-14T12:18:14.139476Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Pairs compared: 10494\nPairs with a different team: 10\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"      raceId  driverId  constructorId_quali  constructorId_result\n6499     937       829                  206                   209\n6500     937       833                  206                   209\n6578     941       829                  206                   209\n6579     941       833                  206                   209\n6597     942       834                  206                   209\n6598     942       829                  206                   209\n6617     943       834                  206                   209\n6618     943       829                  206                   209\n6637     944