g each row.\n- **raceId / driverId:** the race and the driver the row belongs to.\n- **points:** the driver's championship points.\n- **position / positionText:** the driver's rank in the championship, as a number and as text.\n- **wins:** the driver's number of wins.\n\n**What we notice**\n\n1) **Types:** all columns are numbers except `positionText`, so only that column could hide a placeholder; the audit checks it.\n\n2) **The standings of a race already include that race:** this table works like a league table updated after every race. For race 18, the points here (10, 8, 6, 5, 4) are exactly the points the same drivers scored in that race in `results`, and driver 1 already has 1 win, the win from that race. So the standings attached to a race are the standings *after* it, and using them to predict that same race would be leakage.\n\n3) **How to use them safely:** to predict a race, we take each driver's standings from the previous race, which are known before the race starts. The F1 introduction calls this shifting standings to \"standings before this race\". Used this way, they show how well a driver has done in the season so far, which makes them a good candidate for a form feature in Section 3.\n\n4) **More rows than `results`:** this table has 34,863 rows, while `results` has 26,759. So some drivers appear in the standings of races they did not enter, probably because a driver keeps a place in the championship even when they miss a race. We keep this in mind when attaching standings to result rows in Section 3.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**constructor_standings**","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"df = raw[\"constructor_standings\"]\ndf.info()\ndisplay(df.head())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-14T13:03:09.603044Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-14T13:03:09.603721Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-14T13:03:09.621764Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-14T13:03:09.60369Z","shell.execut